 mysterious father  men of one  fushiguru that and all of the world  building in regards to tengen who is a  fascinating figure all  in good time though because we are going  to begin in the only place i know how i  suspect that in an alternate reality we  could have had a version of jiu jitsu  that just featured gojo as the  straight-up protagonist  because this whole arc was about 15 or  so chapters without seeing yuji and  everything was  honestly it was pretty fine go driver  comfortably held up that mantle in yuji  stead and it was fun to watch him  develop into the gojo we know today  because  as flippant and arguably reckless as  gojo is in the monday his student years  were certainly  much much worse asking all sorts of  questions like a curtain's even  necessary does it even matter if regular  people see all this weird crap  as well as the whole idea of how painful  it is to look out for the week so he  certainly had a more simplistic view of  the world almost  optimistic really only truly developing  his revolutionary mentality towards the  end of the arc and while it wasn't much  there was some information given  regarding gojo's specific curse  techniques named limitless and six  eyes the combination of which are  apparently what makes gojo the  ridiculous curse boss he is  although six size in particular wasn't  expanded on and as a result intrigues me  greatly what we did get to do though was  a deep dive into limitless with all the  neutral blues reds and  purple business which was great because  we've seen gojo use these curse  techniques in the past but knowing how  they operate is always what i crave  and intriguingly this also delves  further into reversed cursed energy  which  put more simply can be called positive  energy which is apparently achieved  through a simple yet impractical  mathematic equation of negative times  negative equals positive which is an  idea that i think i sort of feared at  the beginning of jujutsukaisen i really  liked that this world dealt exclusively  with negative energy and theoretically  to become more powerful you would just  need to invoke more and more negativity  so higher risk for higher reward but at  the very least i do like that  right now anyway positive energy cannot  be achieved on its own it needs to be  summoned directly from the negative  and that concept in and of itself really  does sue gojo who seems to have chosen  the path  of embracing negative energy to change  the world around him as opposed to say  ghetto who comes to the conclusion that  we should just stop curses and negative  energy manifesting in the first place  how  you ask well mass genocide of course but  i do like that contrast  getting into ghetto i got quite a  legitimate shock when i saw him pop  up as a student so damn casually i mean  i know it's been implied or perhaps even  outright stated that he was a former  jiu-jitsu student i  honestly don't remember but seeing his  family smiley face was the exact  moment where i set up and went right  we'll in for a bit of a story here  because this does serve as the  villainous origin story of ghetto which  is perfectly contrasted against gojo  because as much as they may disagree  about the basics these two were very  much on the same page for quite some  time and the prime example i would cite  of that is that they both came to the  conclusion that they would let rico  choose whether or not she wanted to  merge with tengen so both displaying  this rebellious yet merciful and  compassionate nature and quite honestly  it's a bit of a jarring shift with  ghetto one that i'm not 100  sure i buy like it takes an awful lot to  transform someone from the paragon of  humanity determined to protect the weak  and all of  the good things to decide that killing a  large majority of humans on the planet  is the ultimate solution although i will  admit that it was quite disturbing to  see all of those religious cultures  supporting the death of an innocent  young girl  and deal with the death of a first-year  student and then stumble into the  village where all of the people wanted  to kill the curse-wielding little girls  so you know what sure it's the more  selfish self-preservation path where  ghetto would rather sacrifice the bulk  of humanity to protect sorcerers rather  than sacrificing sorceress to protect  the rest of the ungrateful swine on this  planet and i don't know it's hard to  grapple with because these specific  experiences ghetto is undergone do point  to this darker and perhaps even  unsalvageable aspect of humans  so i mean why bother protecting them if  they are the sole cause of everything  that's wrong in the first place and you  know the more i talk about it the more  i'm actually convincing myself of  ghetto's motivations so even writing  this out is a bit of a journey but still  let's say the ghetto is on the right  track you would still need to rid the  world of humans pretty much all at once  otherwise you're just perpetuating  negative energy because the surviving  family and friends and just other people  will fester into these gigantic negative  energy batteries like jinpe and that's  to say nothing of the people who are  both not sorcerers and also  don't output much negative energy like  bread girl and naomi's flashback i mean  why would we kill bread girl she is  amazing and she makes us delicious  delicious bread well you just cast me  yuko  or jinpei's mother fushigoro's sister  yuji's grandfather  all of these people who have a net  positive effect on the world anyway  we've probably spoken about this a bit  too much i do like that i  kind of understand where ghetto is  coming from now even if i do think his  ultimate  ideology is simplistic and just a little  poorly thought out and rather twistedly  he's very much become like the cultist  he's so despised but we do need to get  into our third star player a very  unexpected presence from toji fujiguru  when he was first brought up i thought  it would be for some sort of eventual  conflict between he and megami but i  think he was pretty perfectly dealt with  here he at once represents the great  potential of humanity going forward as  well as the profound danger he does the  former by being apparently the only  successful case of a human's cursed  energy being reduced to absolute zero  via a fun  heavenly restriction so that's quite  handy and perhaps a potential path  forward to rid the world of negative  energy  although perhaps even more wildly  impractical than even ghetto solution  but that's really what made toji so  fascinating because he presented us with  the antithesis of jujutsu sorcery  someone who was able to completely  counter those forces without a single  drop  of his own cursed energy and it really  does highlight the fact that jujutsu  sorcerers and curse wielders in general  may be far too  over reliant on that energy and not at  all prepared for the more basic of human  challenges which is something that we  have seen portrayed through both yuji  and maki with their raw strength and  speed but here we saw a tip  top source referral victim in gojo which  was pretty amazing  for the very first time introduced  kaisen we have confirmed that gojo  is indeed mortal no matter how  ridiculous his sorcery is he can still  be brought down by the right level of  planning which i imagine is going to be  an important factor in going forward  with the whole plot to seal gojo's  shenanigans  back to doji though i really liked his  whole gimmick of facing off against  curse users by wielding a spirit that  not only holds all of the funky weapons  but even om nom noms itself it was a  really cool idea and i'm kind of sad  that toji did meet his demise here if  only because i wanted to see more of  that spirit in use  all in all though toji did serve as a  very solid antagonist i would even go so  far as to say that he has the best  antagonist arc to date although to be  fair i suppose he 